TAM-FILE -- TERMINAL FILE MANAGER

tam-file is a terminal file manager written in C11, depending only on the
standard C libraries (POSIX libc) and libtam
(https://github.com/greenermoose/libtam).

It begins as a ground-up C11 rewrite of Yazi
(https://github.com/sxyazi/yazi) -- matching Yazi's fast, non-blocking
three-pane Miller-column UX, async task model, and Vim-like ergonomics --
before diverging to add Tamlinux-native features and omit unnecessary
complexity.

Status: Planning & initial repository setup (pre-v0.1.0). No C source files
have been written yet; see ROADMAP.md for the phased design and
implementation plan.

Follows the tam-idol 0.2.0 standard
(https://github.com/greenermoose/tam-idol).


DESIGN PHILOSOPHY

- Pure C11 + libtam Only:
  Links statically against libtam.a (tam_str, tam_fs, tam_fs_tree,
  tam_path, tam_exec, tam_ini, tam_tty, tam_key, tam_uni). No Rust
  toolchain, no Lua runtime, no ncurses, and no heavy crate graph.

- Fast Cold Start & Sub-Megabyte RSS:
  Designed for 0-2 ms startup and minimal resident memory on both modern
  terminal emulators (foot) and bare Linux virtual consoles (TERM=linux).

- Safe, Atomic, Non-Blocking File Operations:
  Built on tam_fs_tree (tam_fs_clone_or_copy for extent sharing,
  tam_fs_remove_tree, tam_fs_walk) and controlled child processes via
  tam_exec so large copies, previews, and searches never freeze the UI.

- Clone First, Then Diverge:
  Phase 0-4 establish core parity with Yazi's daily-driver file manager
  workflow; Phase 5+ diverge to tailor tam-file for Tamlinux (tight
  tam-edit/tam-data/tam_portal integration and focused C-only extensibility).


CORE FEATURES (PLANNED)

- Three-pane Miller-column layout (parent / current / preview) with
  configurable column ratios and multi-tab support.
- Vim-like navigation (h/j/k/l, gg/G, Ctrl+u/Ctrl+d), visual/multi-select
  mode, cross-directory yank/cut/paste, bulk rename via $EDITOR (tam-edit),
  and trash/permanent delete.
- Non-blocking background task scheduler with live progress indicator
  (copy, move, delete, chmod, archive).
- Scrollable text, directory, archive, and media previews (delegating
  syntax/PDF/image rendering via tam_exec to external tools like chafa,
  pdftotext, or file when present, with clean text fallback on TERM=linux).
- Fast jump & search integration via tam_exec (fd, ripgrep, fzf, zoxide).
- Shell-cwd-on-exit (--cwd-file) and file-chooser mode (--chooser-file).


PLANNED CONFIGURATION & PATHS

Configuration follows XDG conventions via tam_xdg_path:

  $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/tamlinux/tam-file/tam-file.ini   UI, previewers, openers
  $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/tamlinux/tam-file/keymap.ini     Modal keybindings
  $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/tamlinux/tam-file/theme.ini      Colors and icons/ASCII


BUILD PREREQUISITES (PLANNED)

- C11 compiler (gcc or clang)
- POSIX libc (glibc or musl) and GNU Make
- libtam (libtam.a)

See ROADMAP.md for the milestone breakdown from v0.1.0 to v1.0.0 and
ideas/yazi.md for architectural notes from Yazi.


